  "slug": "linked-dysfunction-7444",
  "summary": "Recent preprints link autism, schizophrenia, and Parkinsonian models to altered striatal dopamine and cholinergic circuit function.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is altered neuromodulatory control of striatal output by dopamine and acetylcholine. In a TSHZ3-linked autism model, cholinergic-neuron deletion of Tshz3 reduces M1 muscarinic transmission onto striatal projection neurons, abolishing D1 versus D2 excitability differences. A 3q29 deletion model of schizophrenia risk shows elevated evoked dorsal striatal dopamine release via a PKC-mediated mechanism. In a 6-OHDA Parkinson's model, prolonged D1 receptor signaling loss makes direct-pathway neurons hyperresponsive to dopamine. Analysis of 55 psychiatric-patient dopamine transporter variants resolves seven phenotypic classes of dysfunction. Neuron-specific Aralar/AGC1 knockout adds a metabolic dimension, with dopaminergic and redox deficits rescued by β-hydroxybutyrate. The items differ in disease context and mechanism; the shared element is perturbation of striatal dopamine and cholinergic control across genetic, pharmacological, and variant-level models.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
